1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,028
Solve x cubed minus two x minus five equals zero.

2
00:00:05,028 --> 00:00:07,957
There is no neat formula to factorise it.

3
00:00:07,957 --> 00:00:15,806
But a computer can home in on the root: guess three, follow the tangent down to the axis, and use where it lands as the next guess.

4
00:00:16,033 --> 00:00:27,428
Two point three six, then two point one two seven, then two point zero nine five, and the digits soon stop changing: the root is two point zero nine four six.

5
00:00:27,428 --> 00:00:34,449
Numerical methods like this solve problems that algebra cannot, and the art is knowing how accurate the answer is.

6
00:00:39,933 --> 00:00:45,270
Welcome to Class 12 Mathematics, Unit 15: Computational Methods.

7
00:00:47,667 --> 00:00:48,775
Four parts.

8
00:00:48,775 --> 00:00:51,978
Gauss elimination for systems of equations.

9
00:00:51,978 --> 00:00:54,257
The Gauss Seidel iteration.

10
00:00:54,257 --> 00:00:56,913
Linear programming by the simplex method.

11
00:00:56,913 --> 00:01:02,035
And how accurate a computed answer is: Newton Raphson and the integration rules.

12
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:12,756
Gauss elimination is the systematic version of eliminating by hand, which is why computers use it.

13
00:01:12,756 --> 00:01:14,891
Write the augmented matrix.

14
00:01:14,891 --> 00:01:22,640
Use the first pivot to clear the first column: subtract twice row one from row two, and three times row one from row three.

15
00:01:22,867 --> 00:01:28,455
Then the second pivot clears the second column: add twice row two to row three.

16
00:01:28,455 --> 00:01:30,536
The matrix is now triangular.

17
00:01:30,767 --> 00:01:36,591
Back substitute from the bottom: z is three, y is two, x is one.

18
00:01:36,800 --> 00:01:40,630
If a pivot turns out to be zero, swap in a lower row.

19
00:01:40,630 --> 00:01:46,986
In practice, computers always swap in the largest available pivot, to keep rounding errors small.

20
00:01:48,200 --> 00:01:49,397
Recognise the system.

21
00:01:49,600 --> 00:01:54,437
These are the equations we just solved, with every right side multiplied by ten.

22
00:01:54,667 --> 00:02:01,758
So every unknown is ten times bigger: pens ten rupees, notebooks twenty, erasers thirty.

23
00:02:08,133 --> 00:02:11,819
Gauss Seidel takes another route: iteration.

24
00:02:11,819 --> 00:02:15,049
Solve each equation for its own unknown.

25
00:02:15,049 --> 00:02:19,267
Start from a guess, nought, nought, nought, and keep improving.

26
00:02:19,267 --> 00:02:25,491
It is guaranteed to converge when each diagonal coefficient beats the others in its row.

27
00:02:25,491 --> 00:02:28,514
Here twenty beats one plus two, and so on.

28
00:02:28,733 --> 00:02:36,729
Each new value is used immediately: the new y uses the x just found, and the new z uses both.

29
00:02:36,729 --> 00:02:38,709
That is what makes it Gauss Seidel.

30
00:02:38,709 --> 00:02:43,813
Watch the digits settle: by the fourth or fifth round they no longer change.

31
00:02:43,813 --> 00:02:48,002
x is one, y is minus one, z is one.

32
00:02:49,233 --> 00:02:50,385
Check dominance first.

33
00:02:50,600 --> 00:02:52,460
The first two rows fail the test.

34
00:02:52,667 --> 00:02:56,364
Reorder so each big coefficient sits on the diagonal.

35
00:02:56,567 --> 00:03:00,863
Now every row is dominant, and the iteration will converge.

36
00:03:06,433 --> 00:03:07,763
Linear programming.

37
00:03:07,763 --> 00:03:14,234
Make as much profit Z equals three x plus five y as you can, under two limits.

38
00:03:14,234 --> 00:03:18,453
The limits cut out a region, the feasible region, a polygon.

39
00:03:18,453 --> 00:03:23,205
Slide the profit line outward: the last point it touches is a corner.

40
00:03:23,205 --> 00:03:25,471
The best answer is always at a corner.

41
00:03:25,700 --> 00:03:32,017
The simplex method finds that corner without drawing anything, which matters when there are many variables.

42
00:03:32,017 --> 00:03:40,090
It starts at the origin and walks along edges from corner to corner, improving Z each time, until no step helps.

43
00:03:40,090 --> 00:03:46,514
Here: nought nought, then nought two, then three one, where Z is fourteen.

44
00:03:47,733 --> 00:03:48,750
The rules.

45
00:03:48,750 --> 00:03:55,091
Turn each inequality into an equation with a slack variable, the unused capacity.

46
00:03:55,091 --> 00:03:59,141
Choose the pivot column with the most negative number in the Z row.

47
00:03:59,141 --> 00:04:04,076
Choose the pivot row with the smallest ratio of right side to column entry.

48
00:04:04,076 --> 00:04:06,133
Pivot, as in Gauss Jordan.

49
00:04:06,133 --> 00:04:09,331
Repeat until the Z row has no negatives.

50
00:04:10,533 --> 00:04:19,975
In the first tableau, minus five is the most negative, so y enters; the smallest ratio is two, so s two leaves.

51
00:04:19,975 --> 00:04:22,097
One more pivot brings in x.

52
00:04:22,097 --> 00:04:32,635
In the final tableau the Z row has no negatives: x is three, y is one, and the maximum Z is fourteen, the same corner we found by sliding the line.

53
00:04:33,867 --> 00:04:34,761
A real problem.

54
00:04:35,100 --> 00:04:39,603
Set up: profit in hundreds of rupees, and the two hour limits.

55
00:04:39,833 --> 00:04:43,124
Two pivots: first x enters, then y.

56
00:04:43,333 --> 00:04:48,921
Twelve chairs and six tables, for thirteen thousand two hundred rupees.

57
00:04:48,921 --> 00:04:50,617
Both workshops are fully booked.

58
00:04:56,200 --> 00:05:03,623
Newton Raphson, from the cold open: x n plus one is x n minus f over f dash.

59
00:05:03,623 --> 00:05:07,308
Near a root, the error is squared at every step.

60
00:05:07,308 --> 00:05:14,952
Watch the error column: about a tenth, then five thousandths, then two hundred thousandths, then two ten-billionths.

61
00:05:14,952 --> 00:05:18,828
The number of correct digits roughly doubles each time.

62
00:05:20,033 --> 00:05:22,130
Square roots, the ancient way.

63
00:05:22,333 --> 00:05:26,686
The formula becomes: average x and five over x.

64
00:05:26,900 --> 00:05:34,065
From two: two point two five, then two point two three six one one one, then the digits freeze.

65
00:05:34,267 --> 00:05:38,151
Root five is two point two three six oh seven.

66
00:05:40,167 --> 00:05:41,692
Numerical integration.

67
00:05:41,692 --> 00:05:44,426
Split the area into four strips.

68
00:05:44,426 --> 00:05:50,169
The trapezium rule replaces the curve by straight chords: point seven eight two eight.

69
00:05:50,169 --> 00:05:56,939
Simpson's rule fits parabolas through three points at a time: point seven eight five three nine.

70
00:05:56,939 --> 00:05:58,788
The true value is pi over four.

71
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:04,314
The trapezium error shrinks like h squared; Simpson's like h to the fourth.

72
00:06:04,314 --> 00:06:10,095
Here, with the same five values, Simpson is about four hundred times more accurate.

73
00:06:10,095 --> 00:06:14,945
Halve h, and the trapezium error falls by four, Simpson's by sixteen.

74
00:06:17,367 --> 00:06:18,137
Pause and think.

75
00:06:25,433 --> 00:06:31,172
Diagonally dominant: each diagonal coefficient is bigger than the sum of the others in its row.

76
00:06:33,167 --> 00:06:33,602
One more.

77
00:06:41,233 --> 00:06:43,813
The most negative entry in the Z row.

78
00:06:43,813 --> 00:06:46,666
The smallest ratio then picks the pivot row.

79
00:06:48,833 --> 00:06:49,902
Quick recap.

80
00:06:49,902 --> 00:06:52,949
Gauss elimination and back substitution.

81
00:06:52,949 --> 00:06:56,934
Gauss Seidel iteration for diagonally dominant systems.

82
00:06:56,934 --> 00:07:00,436
The simplex method walking from corner to corner.

83
00:07:00,436 --> 00:07:04,131
And the accuracy of Newton Raphson and the integration rules.

84
00:07:08,300 --> 00:07:10,281
That was computational methods.

85
00:07:10,281 --> 00:07:16,361
Next, the alternative unit, mechanics: forces, equilibrium and motion.

86
00:07:16,361 --> 00:07:16,935
See you there.

